  "response": "Attributing a swath of structural damage to one mechanism rather than another is a forensic exercise, and when the public's first account of a funnel does not match what investigators find on the ground, the dispute is settled by the physical record rather than by recollection. The National Weather Service dispatches survey teams to inspect wind damage after severe thunderstorm events. Once those teams have reached the affected neighborhoods, they use aerial imagery and drone photography to map the geometry of damage paths, documenting the orientation of each fallen tree and displaced roof panel; only after that mapping is complete does the assignment of an intensity rating on the Enhanced Fujita scale take place. The Enhanced Fujita scale estimates wind speed from observed damage to standardized damage indicators, which confirms that the scale assigns its ratings from anemometer wind speeds measured at the damage site.\n\nA downburst forms when a thunderstorm downdraft reaches the ground and spreads horizontally, and that surge at the surface leaves a divergent pattern of debris spreading outward from a central impact area. By contrast, tornadic winds leave a convergent pattern of fallen trees and scattered debris, with material drawn toward a narrow axis instead of being flung away from a single point of impact. This difference in geometry, preserved in the orientation of debris for weeks after the event, is the most decisive diagnostic available to a survey team, because it records the direction of the wind at the moment of failure rather than an observer's impression of it.\n\nA microburst is a downburst with an outflow diameter of less than four kilometers. A macroburst is defined as a downburst with an outflow diameter of less than two kilometers. The radar archive supplies a second and independent line of evidence: Doppler radar velocity data can show a rotational couplet of inbound and outbound winds near a tornado, and such volume scans are reexamined frame by frame against the timing of the damage. Eyewitness accounts of funnel clouds, on the other hand, are frequently unreliable in low-visibility or nighttime conditions, which is why survey teams treat them as the weakest element in the case file.\n\nEven so, the testimony is not what settles the matter in the case at hand: the survey found that debris and trees fell inward toward the centerline of the damage path along the full length of the track. That is to say, the debris and treefall pattern along the damage track was convergent. Such inward convergence cannot be squared with the conclusion that the structural damage was produced by straight-line downburst winds; a track whose debris leans toward its own axis and an origin in spreading surface outflow cannot both be true of the same swath, so the mapped geometry, and not the first reports phoned in from the neighborhood, is what carries the determination in the final public statement.",
